{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Hecke.TauSymmetrizing","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Hecke.TauSymmetrizing","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Hecke.TwMul","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.InvMonomialDet"],"declarations":8,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.TwBasis.tauGram_det_ne_zero_of_structure_constants","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.TwBasis.tauGram_det_ne_zero_of_structure_constants","kind":"theorem","line":139,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/TauSymmetrizing.lean#L139-L152","doc":"**Hence `H_n(q)` is Frobenius for every `q ≠ 0`** — the `τ`-Gram is\nnondegenerate.  Note this is *not* semisimplicity, which fails at roots of\nunity; see the module docstring. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.TwBasis.tauGram_det_of_structure_constants","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.TwBasis.tauGram_det_of_structure_constants","kind":"theorem","line":119,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/TauSymmetrizing.lean#L119-L137","doc":"**Given the structure-constant identity, the Gram determinant is a sign\ntimes a product of `q`-powers.**\n\nThe hypothesis is Geck–Pfeiffer's `τ(T_v T_w) = δ_{w,v⁻¹} q^{ℓ(v)}`; it is a\nhypothesis, not an axiom, and the conclusion is a genuine implication.  Once\nthe hypothesis is discharged the conclusion becomes unconditional with no\nchange to this proof. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.TwBasis.tauGram_apply","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.TwBasis.tauGram_apply","kind":"theorem","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/TauSymmetrizing.lean#L116-L117","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.TwBasis.tauGram","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.TwBasis.tauGram","kind":"def","line":111,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/TauSymmetrizing.lean#L111-L114","doc":"**The Gram matrix of `(x, y) ↦ τ(x y)` in the `T_w` basis.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.TwBasis.tau_Tw_mul_Tw_of_length_add","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.TwBasis.tau_Tw_mul_Tw_of_length_add","kind":"theorem","line":98,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/TauSymmetrizing.lean#L98-L109","doc":"**The identity holds on the length-additive locus**, without assumption:\nwhere the lengths add, `T_v · T_w = T_{wv}` and `τ` reads off `δ_{wv,1}`.\n\nThis is consistent with the conjectured `δ_{w,v⁻¹} q^{ℓ(v)}` rather than a\nproof of it: on this locus `w = v⁻¹` forces `ℓ(v) + ℓ(w) = ℓ(1) = 0`, so the\n`q`-power is `q^0 = 1`.  The locus therefore never sees the `q`-powers, which\nis exactly why the general identity needs the descent induction instead. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.TwBasis.tau_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.TwBasis.tau_one","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/TauSymmetrizing.lean#L94-L96","doc":"`τ(1) = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.TwBasis.tau_Tw","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.TwBasis.tau_Tw","kind":"theorem","line":88,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/TauSymmetrizing.lean#L88-L92","doc":"**`τ(T_w) = δ_{w,1}`** — the defining property. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.TwBasis.tau","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.TwBasis.tau","kind":"def","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/TauSymmetrizing.lean#L80-L86","doc":"**The canonical trace `τ`**: the `T_1`-coordinate in the `T_w` basis.\n\nThis is the functional Geck–Pfeiffer characterise by `τ(T_w) = δ_{w,1}`; the\ncharacterisation is `tau_Tw` below.  That it is a *trace* — `τ(xy) = τ(yx)`\n— is the open half, and is nowhere assumed here. ","signatureChunk":0}]}